</figure>

I say that the remainder {EB} is to the remainder {FD} as the whole {AB} is to the whole {CD}.

Since {AB} is to {CD} as {AE} is to {CF}, therefore {AE} is the same part or parts of {CF} as {AB} is of {CD}. Therefore the remainder {EB} is the same part or parts of {FD} that {AB} is of {CD}. [!just VII.Def.20; VII.7, VII.8]

Therefore {EB} is to {FD} as {AB} is to {CD}. [!just VII.Def.20]

Therefore, *if a whole is to a whole as a subtracted number is to a subtracted number, then the remainder is to the remainder as the whole is to the whole.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide:

This proposition is the numerical analogue of proposition @V.19 for general magnitudes. Symbolically,

<center>if <i>a</i> : <i>c</i> = <i>e</i> : <i>f,</i> then <i>a – e</i> : <i>c – f</i> = <i>a</i> : <i>c.</i></center>

This proposition is used in @IX.35.
